// Journey four again, as a process rather than as a system: what happens to a
// logged set between the thumb and the database, written so somebody who has
// never heard of a token or a partition can follow it.
//
// Five pages. `loggerJourney` is the short version and the place to start;
// each of its stations links on to the page that opens it up. The other four
// are the ordinary tap in full, the tap with no signal, the tap from an
// out-of-date screen, and finishing the workout.
//
// Every page uses the same running example — 100 kg for 5 on a squat that
// already has two sets — so the numbers on one page can be checked against
// the next.
//
// The stations are declared in ../logger.c4. The vocabulary is held fixed
// across all five: the token is "the pass", Easy Auth is "the door check",
// the guard count is "the count", Cosmos is "the training log".
views {

  // =======================================================================
  // The short version
  // =======================================================================

  dynamic view loggerJourney {
    title 'Logging a set — the short version'
    description '
      What happens between tapping "Log set" and the set being safely stored,
      in nine steps. Start here.

      Three ideas carry the whole design, and every other page in this group
      is one of them in detail:

      1. The screen does not wait. The set appears the instant it is tapped,
         and a queue in the page does the waiting instead.

      2. Every set carries a count — "this exercise had two sets before this
         one". The database only adds the set while that is still true. That
         one rule is why sending the same set twice can never log it twice,
         and why the queue can keep retrying on bad wifi without fear.

      3. Nothing on the request says whose log it is. The pass from signing
         in does, and the server reads it from there and nowhere else.

      Click a step to read more about it. The steps that link onwards open
      the page that covers them in detail.
    '

    user -> gym.screen 'Taps "Log set": 100 kg × 5' {
      notes '
        A squat that already has two sets logged. This is the third.
      '
    }

    gym.screen -> gym.outbox 'Shows the set straight away, and queues it with the count: "this squat had 2 sets"' {
      notes '
        The row is on screen before anything has left the phone. The count
        is taken from what the screen shows, which already includes every
        earlier tap — so by the time this set reaches the server, the count
        is the one the server holds.
      '
      navigateTo loggerTap
    }

    gym.outbox -> gym.signin 'Asks for a valid pass' {
      notes '
        Asked for on every send rather than kept, so it is never the expired
        one an hour into a session.
      '
    }

    gym.signin -> entra.gymlog 'Renews the pass, only when it is about to run out' {
      notes '
        Most taps never get this far — the pass is reused until near its
        expiry. How the pass was obtained in the first place is `gymSignIn`.
      '
      navigateTo gymSignIn
    }

    gym.outbox -> api.gate 'Sends the set over the internet, with the pass' {
      notes '
        If this does not get through — the dead spot by the squat rack — the
        set stays on screen and in the queue, and is sent again.
      '
      navigateTo loggerOffline
    }

    api.gate -> api.sets 'The pass is good: lets the request in' {
      notes '
        A missing or expired pass is turned away here, before any of our code
        runs at all.
      '
    }

    api.sets -> cosmos 'Adds the set to your log — only if the squat still has 2 sets' {
      notes '
        Whose log is read off the pass. The "only if" is the count, checked
        by the database in the same step as the write, so nothing can slip
        in between.
      '
      navigateTo loggerStale
    }

    gym.outbox <- api.sets 'Recorded' {
      notes '
        Or "already recorded", which means the same thing: this set had been
        sent before and the first attempt landed. Either way it is success.
      '
    }

    gym.screen <- gym.outbox 'Header changes to "Saved 19:42"' {
      notes '
        The queue moves on to the next change, if there is one. When the
        workout is done, Finish waits for this queue to be empty — see
        `loggerFinish`.
      '
      navigateTo loggerFinish
    }

    autoLayout LeftRight 120 90

    style entra, entra.gymlog {
      color muted
    }
  }

  // =======================================================================
  // The ordinary tap, in full
  // =======================================================================

  dynamic view loggerTap {
    title 'Logging a set — every step, when all goes well'
    description '
      The same tap as `loggerJourney`, with nothing left out. Read it top to
      bottom; each arrow is one thing happening, in order.

      Two steps here surprise people. The first is the browser asking
      permission before every send: a page may only send a signed-in request
      to another website once that website says it accepts them, and the
      platform cannot be told to let the browser remember the answer, so it
      asks every time. The second is the set handler never being told whose
      log to write to. It works that out from the pass, because anything the
      request says about itself could have been typed by anybody.

      The whole round trip is normally well under a second, and the lifter
      sees none of it — the row was on screen at step two.
    '

    variant sequence

    user -> gym.screen 'Taps "Log set": 100 kg × 5, effort 8' {
      notes 'Effort (RPE) is optional, between 5 and 10 on a half step.'
    }

    gym.screen -> gym.screen 'Draws the new row and updates the totals at once'

    gym.screen -> gym.outbox 'Queues the set: squat, 100 kg, 5 reps, effort 8 — and "this squat had 2 sets"' {
      notes '
        Changes go out one at a time, oldest first. If an earlier change is
        still on its way, this one waits behind it, because its count assumes
        that earlier change has already landed.
      '
    }

    gym.outbox -> gym.signin 'Needs a pass'

    gym.outbox <- gym.signin 'Here is a valid one (renewed with Entra if it was about to expire)'

    gym.outbox -> api.gate 'Browser asks first: may this page send signed-in requests to you?' {
      notes '
        The CORS preflight. Answered by the platform, before the door check
        and before any of our code. It costs one extra round trip per tap and
        cannot be switched off from our side.
      '
    }

    gym.outbox <- api.gate 'Yes — gym.nygard.dev is on the list'

    gym.outbox -> api.gate 'The set itself, with the pass attached'

    api.gate -> api.gate 'Checks the pass: genuine, from our tenant, made for GymLog, and obtained by GymLog' {
      notes '
        Fail any of these and the answer is a 401 or 403 from the platform,
        and the page asks the lifter to sign in again. Nothing reaches the
        set handler.
      '
    }

    api.gate -> api.sets 'Lets it in, with the person the pass belongs to'

    api.sets -> api.sets 'Reads whose log this is from the pass, and checks the numbers are sensible' {
      notes '
        Weight between 0 and the limit, at least one rep, effort on a half
        step. A request with nonsense in it is answered with a 400 that
        explains which value, and nothing is written.
      '
    }

    api.sets -> cosmos 'In your log, on today’s session: add this set to the squat — only if it still has 2' {
      notes '
        One write. No read first: the "only if" is checked by the database
        itself as part of the write, so two phones logging at once cannot
        both slip in at position three.
      '
    }

    api.sets <- cosmos 'Done — the squat now has 3 sets'

    gym.outbox <- api.sets 'Recorded: set 3 of the squat'

    gym.screen <- gym.outbox 'Header: "Saved 19:42"'

    gym.outbox -> gym.outbox 'Next change in the queue, if any'
  }

  // =======================================================================
  // No signal
  // =======================================================================

  dynamic view loggerOffline {
    title 'Logging a set with no signal'
    description '
      Gym wifi drops out. This page is what the lifter sees when it does, and
      why it is safe to keep tapping.

      The set stays on screen and stays in the queue. The queue tries again
      after one second, then two, four, eight, fifteen and thirty — about a
      minute in all, which outlasts most dead spots without leaving a change
      retrying long after anybody is looking. Meanwhile the header says how
      many changes are waiting, and anything tapped in the meantime joins the
      back of the queue.

      The tricky case is a set that did arrive but whose answer was lost on
      the way back. Sending it again is safe because of the count: the server
      sees the squat already has three sets, not two, finds that the third is
      exactly this set, and answers "already recorded". One set, not two.

      If a minute passes with no signal, or the session is closed while
      changes are still waiting, the queue gives up honestly: the unsaved
      changes come back off the screen and a banner says so. The screen never
      claims a set is logged when it is not.
    '

    variant sequence

    user -> gym.screen 'Taps "Log set": 100 kg × 5'

    gym.screen -> gym.outbox 'Shows the row at once; queues it with "this squat had 2 sets"'

    gym.outbox -> api.sets 'Sends the set' {
      notes '
        The pass and the door check are left out of this page — they happen
        exactly as on `loggerTap`, on every attempt.
      '
    }

    api.sets -> cosmos 'Adds the set — the squat had 2, so it goes in'

    api.sets <- cosmos 'Done: 3 sets'

    gym.outbox <- api.sets 'Recorded… but the answer is lost on the way back' {
      notes '
        From the phone this looks the same as the set never arriving at all.
        It cannot tell the two apart, and it does not need to.
      '
    }

    gym.screen <- gym.outbox 'Header: "1 set waiting to save"'

    user -> gym.screen 'Keeps training, taps the next set' {
      notes '
        Allowed, and safe. The new set joins the queue behind the first one
        and waits for it, so it is never sent with a count the server does
        not hold yet.
      '
    }

    gym.screen -> gym.outbox 'Queues set 4 behind it; header: "2 sets waiting to save"'

    gym.outbox -> gym.outbox 'Waits 1 s, then 2 s, then 4 s… up to about a minute in all'

    gym.outbox -> api.sets 'Signal is back: sends set 3 again, still saying "had 2 sets"'

    api.sets -> cosmos 'Add this set — only if the squat still has 2'

    api.sets <- cosmos 'Refused: it has 3' {
      notes '
        The database refuses rather than adding a duplicate. The handler then
        looks once at what is stored, to tell "this already landed" apart from
        "this screen is out of date".
      '
    }

    api.sets -> cosmos 'Looks: is the third set exactly 100 kg × 5?'

    api.sets <- cosmos 'Yes'

    gym.outbox <- api.sets 'Already recorded — the first attempt landed' {
      notes '
        Treated as success, because it is. Nothing to undo, nothing to merge,
        and no rule to write about which of two answers to believe.
      '
    }

    gym.outbox -> api.sets 'Set 4, saying "had 3 sets"'

    gym.outbox <- api.sets 'Recorded'

    gym.screen <- gym.outbox 'Header: "Saved 19:44"'
  }

  // =======================================================================
  // An out-of-date screen
  // =======================================================================

  dynamic view loggerStale {
    title 'Logging a set from an out-of-date screen'
    description '
      The phone believed the squat had two sets, but the stored session has
      moved on without it — most often because the same workout was changed
      on another device, such as the planner open on a laptop.

      The count is what catches it. The set is counted from a screen that no
      longer matches what is stored, so the database refuses it, nothing is
      written, and the server answers with what the count really is.

      The page does not show that as an error. It throws away the queued
      changes that were counted from the same stale screen, fetches the
      session as it really is, and shows a short notice that it reloaded. The
      lifter looks at the fresh screen and logs again from there.

      This is the one case where a tap visibly does something other than
      what was tapped, which is why it says so out loud.
    '

    variant sequence

    user -> gym.screen 'Taps "Log set": 100 kg × 5'

    gym.screen -> gym.outbox 'Shows the row; queues it with "this squat had 2 sets"'

    gym.outbox -> api.sets 'Sends the set'

    api.sets -> cosmos 'Add this set — only if the squat still has 2'

    api.sets <- cosmos 'Refused: it has 4' {
      notes '
        Someone — usually the same lifter on another screen — added or
        removed sets since this phone last looked.
      '
    }

    api.sets -> cosmos 'Looks: did this exact set already land as the third?'

    api.sets <- cosmos 'No'

    gym.outbox <- api.sets 'Out of date: you said 2, it has 4. Nothing was written' {
      notes '
        A 409 "count_mismatch", carrying the real count. Not a failure to
        write — the guard doing its job.
      '
    }

    gym.outbox -> gym.outbox 'Drops everything queued from the same stale screen'

    gym.outbox -> api.sets 'Fetches the whole session as it really is'

    gym.outbox <- api.sets 'The session, with its 4 squat sets'

    gym.screen <- gym.outbox 'Redraws from that, with a notice: "This session had changed elsewhere. Reloaded from the server."'

    user -> gym.screen 'Checks the fresh screen, and logs again if the set is still missing'
  }

  // =======================================================================
  // Finishing
  // =======================================================================

  dynamic view loggerFinish {
    title 'Finishing a workout'
    description '
      The last payload a session sends, and the only one not protected by a
      count — so it is protected by waiting instead.

      Finish holds until the save queue is empty. Filing a workout ahead of a
      set still on its way would file it without that set, so a set in flight
      is waited for (normally a moment), and while anything is being retried
      Finish refuses with a note to try again once the header says saved —
      rather than sitting behind a spinner for a minute of retries.

      Filing is then a single change from "draft" to "submitted", which can
      safely be sent twice. Two small things ride along after it has landed:
      if this day of the block had nothing planned, what was just lifted
      becomes its plan, so next week opens with those exercises already in
      place; and the exercises lifted move to the front of "recently used".
      Neither can undo the filing if it goes wrong.
    '

    variant sequence

    user -> gym.screen 'Taps Finish'

    gym.screen -> gym.outbox 'Is anything still waiting to save?'

    gym.screen <- gym.outbox 'All saved' {
      notes '
        If something were still being retried, the answer would be "2 sets
        still waiting to save. Finish once the header says saved." and
        nothing would be filed.
      '
    }

    gym.screen -> api.sets 'File this workout' {
      notes '
        Strictly, a sibling route in the same API — POST …/submit. Drawn on
        the same box because to the lifter it is the same place.
      '
    }

    api.sets -> cosmos 'Mark the session submitted'

    api.sets <- cosmos 'Done'

    api.sets -> cosmos 'If this day had no plan: make what was just lifted its plan' {
      notes '
        Only an empty plan is ever filled, and only from exercises that got
        at least one set. A plan someone typed in is never overwritten.
      '
    }

    api.sets -> cosmos 'Move the exercises just lifted to the front of "recently used"'

    gym.screen <- api.sets 'Filed — and whether this day just got a plan'

    gym.screen -> user 'The Done screen, with the session totals'
  }
}
